<h1>Hello!</h1>
<p><img src="/img/jpm.jpeg" width="150" class="image-align-left" alt="Black and white photograph of a middle-aged white man with glasses and dark hair, wearing a black blazer and floral-patterened shirt, looking down and to the right."> My name is Jonathan Mitchell. I'm a specialist in disability and accessibility. I work for University of Atypical on a programme that builds knowledge about access in the arts sector, and supports arts organisations to make concrete improvements at their venues and in their practices.</p>

<p>I'm also an independent scholar and critical disability theorist, whose background is in philosophy. I was an <a href="https://research.ie/">Irish Research Council Government of Ireland Postgraduate Scholar</a> in <a href="https://www.ucd.ie/philosophy">University College Dublin School of Philosophy</a>, where I completed a PhD called 'Distributing Disability: Embodied Difference, Technology, and the Human'. I do some occasional lecturing, and teach in a continuing education capacity.</p>

<p>I'm particularly interested in persistent tensions between ordinary, everyday accounts of disability and humanness: the many and varied ways disability gets understood as a lack of, or deviation from, a full or proper humanness. I'm also concerned to develop ethical frameworks that can accomodate disability without relying on overarching standards of humanness (theories that do this often end up expelling some disabled people from their moral universe). My main interest of late, however, is in changing conceptions of monstrosity, particularly in the Medieval and Renaissance periods.</p>

<p>This website used to be a place where I shared works in progress. I have archived some things that have since been published. You can find out about those in my <a href="cv">academic curriculum vitae</a>. A few old presentation texts still live in the <a href="blog">posts</a> section. Some of these contain ideas I was developing at the time, but don't wholeheartedly subscribe to any longer. I'm not working on new ideas, and will record developments here.</p>

I'm on <a href="https://www.instagram.com/uncouthregions/">Instagram</a> and have an <a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4107-7453/">ORCiD profile</a>. You can also <a href="mailto:helloATjonathanmitchellDOTme">send me an email</a>.

